[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language#cite_ref-FOOTNOTERomaine1999a2"Other_changes_such_as_the_spread_and_regularisation_of_do_support_began_in_the_thirteenth_century_and_were_more_or_less_complete_in_the_nineteenth._Although_do_coexisted_with_the_simple_verb_forms_in_negative_statements_from_the_early_ninth_century,_obligatoriness_was_not_complete_until_the_nineteenth._The_increasing_use_of_do_periphrasis_coincides_with_the_fixing_of_SVO_word_order._Not_surprisingly,_do_is_first_widely_used_in_interrogatives,_where_the_word_order_is_disrupted,_and_then_later_spread_to_negatives."_64-0]   [LINK: (Romaine 1999a) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_language#CITEREFRomaine1999a] , p. 2, "Other changes such as the spread and regularisation of do support began in the thirteenth century and were more or less complete in the nineteenth. Although do coexisted with the simple verb forms in negative statements from the early ninth century, obligatoriness was not complete until the nineteenth. The increasing use of do periphrasis coincides with the fixing of SVO word order. Not surprisingly, do is first widely used in interrogatives, where the word order is disrupted, and then later spread to negatives.".

